WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.027
Multi-turn is resent history.

2
00:00:02.307 --> 00:00:03.800
The API is stateless.

3
00:00:04.080 --> 00:00:16.752
It does not remember your previous request, so a “conversation” is something your code builds: after each reply, append the assistant’s turn to your list, append the user’s next turn, and send the whole list again.

4
00:00:17.032 --> 00:00:23.005
The model sees the full history every time, which is exactly why it can refer back to earlier turns.

5
00:00:23.285 --> 00:00:26.165
That design has two consequences you should plan for.

6
00:00:26.445 --> 00:00:37.432
Cost and latency grow with every turn, because you pay for the entire history as input on each request; a 30-turn support chat bills its first message 30 times.

7
00:00:37.712 --> 00:00:48.421
And you control memory completely: you can drop, summarize, or edit old turns, which is powerful and also a way to confuse the model if you rewrite history carelessly.

8
00:00:48.701 --> 00:00:57.555
When you append the assistant’s turn, append the full content list the API returned (all blocks, unchanged), not just the text you extracted.

9
00:00:57.835 --> 00:01:03.403
Current models check that returned blocks such as thinking come back exactly as they were produced.

10
00:01:03.683 --> 00:01:08.760
Prompt caching (week 2) is the standard way to make long, stable prefixes cheap.
